<p>My book <a href="https://www.amazon.com/Painting-Animals-Oil-Simon-Bland/dp/1548897701">Painting Animals in Oil</a> is coming up on its 10-year anniversary, so I thought it might be a good idea to write some blog articles based on the contents. It's something that I've been meaning to do for a long time.</p>
<p>To start, I want to write about one of my favorite topics which is also the first section of the first chapter in the book. This article on ideation or developing ideas was rewritten for a blog audience and is cherry-picked from the original. Obviously, the original text focuses mostly on animals; I've made this version less specific and much shorter.</p>
<p>One problem with trying to write about ideas is that I'm not sure all artists would agree on a definition of <em>idea</em>. It's not something that I've ever discussed with another artist, to be honest. My own view is that it's the something that tells me <em>what</em>, <em>how</em> and <em>why</em> I'm going to paint. It may or may not involve a lightbulb appearing over my head, but more on that below.</p>
<p>The other thing that I want to mention is about the originality of ideas. To paraphrase <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Freed">Arthur Freed</a>, "you don't have to be different to be good, being good is different enough". What I take away from this (and very much put into practice), is that you don't have to reinvent the wheel every time you create something. Usually, ideas come in response to some kind of inspiration, but very occasionally we let ourselves believe that they have been dreamed up out of thin air. The fact is that all creative work is derivative to some extent: we are always building on what has come before and stealing ideas from others.</p>
<h3>Developing an Idea</h3>
<p>My favorite way to come up with ideas is to take a subject and devote some time to play around with it, try out lots of different approaches. My only rule is that anything goes.</p>
<p>Let me show you what one of my ideas might look like:</p>
<p><img alt="A small, abstract painting of a blue triangle shape against a foggy, grey background which represents a sheep" src="/images/2026/SheepStudy-sm.jpg"/></p>
<p>Sketch of an idea for painting a sheep. Approx. 4"x3" oil on linen.</p>
<p>I noodled out this small sketch by thinking about how to build around warm/cool temperature contrasts. It's just the end result of playing around with paint on a scrap of canvas before I started to fully engage with designing a picture. It also helped me to see the picture as being about something other than the subject (more on this below)—in this case a cool triangle set in a foggy, diffuse light.</p>
<p>The biggest benefit of sketching out an idea like this is that it reduces a big-picture-sized problem to a small index-card-sized problem. The small size makes it easier to see the picture as a unified whole and to reduce the design to just a few big shapes. In turn, that makes it easier to create a painting that's not simply a copy of a photograph.</p>
<p>You can do lots of variations with very little effort and I did many before this one (none of them as good, unfortunately), but this stood out because it was so sparse compared to the others. It's also nothing fancy and not entirely original. Nevertheless, this is an idea that I kept working at and which I reused several times for other paintings of sheep.</p>
<h3>The Idea in Abstract</h3>
<p>The idea shown above is built on something other than the sheep: in this case the simple arrangement of a triangle against a gradated background. Something like this:</p>
<p><img alt="Abstract representation of a sheep's head as a triangle set against a gradated background" src="/images/2026/Sheep-Abstract.png"/></p>
<p>The sheep idea as an abstract representation.</p>
<p>Coming at the idea in this abstract way helped me to focus on what I wanted to say. By reducing down to some shapes and light, I didn't get stuck in the sheep's details and could see past the background of the original photograph. Later on, I was able to re-use the idea to create different designs by moving the abstract elements around.</p>
<p>I've shown a couple of different sheep paintings below. You can see that the abstract idea reduces to the same thing—a triangle against a gradated background—in each case.</p>
<p><img alt="Oil painting of a sheep in repose looking directly at the viewer" src="/images/2026/Sheep.jpg"/></p>
<p>I think this was the first sheep painting I did using the idea above.</p>
<p><img alt="OIl painting of a sheep seen from the side. The picture is overall grey and textured." src="/images/2026/Sheep-Gray.jpg"/></p>
<p>Here's another—as I mentioned, I reused the idea many times.</p>
<h3>Putting Into Practice</h3>
<p>I use the term <em>developing ideas</em> for this process because I don't want to make anyone think that an idea must come as a creative spark, i.e. in a flash, all at once. My best ideas have usually come when I've played around with them and I believe that the only way to consistently generate ideas is through work. If you think of an idea as coming at a "lightbulb" moment, the lightbulb almost always appears over my head when I'm making art of one form or another.</p>
<p>My goal is to make time for some kind of playing-around-with-ideas process for all types of subject matter, no matter the source of my inspiration or how complete the picture already is in my mind.</p>
<p>With an idea prepared, the painting process can begin. Before starting a major painting or any portrait, that means I go through a design process that involves trying out different value scales, compositions and color combinations (I cover this extensively in my book). As a rule of thumb, I might spend up to a quarter of a painting’s total effort on design and much of that will be spent on testing out different ways to present these ideas.</p>
<p>Having said all this, your own experience may be different! Your own ideas may arrive at the same moment at which inspiration strikes and you can start painting by the seat of your pants, developing the painting as you go. Many plein air painters work this way, for example, although I think this is one of the reasons why plein air painting is so difficult.</p>
<p>No matter your process, I would encourage you experiment as much as possible. To do that, you could start saving scraps of canvas, old panels and discarded canvases and use them to make small abstract sketches like the one above (I've written about this in the context of <a href="https://portraitsbysimonbland.com/blog/what-to-do-with-old-canvases/">what to do with old canvases</a>). Even if most of those don't go on to become full-blown paintings, there's no substitute for getting into the creative flow.</p>
